When a single generic type argument that contains an intersection (`&`), a
`keyof` operator, an array type (`[]`), or an indexed access type (`["key"]`)
exceeds the print width, it is now broken onto its own indented line — matching
the behavior that already existed for union types. This applies to both variable
declarations and async arrow function return type annotations.

<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```tsx
// Input
const foo: Promise<SomeReallyLongTypeName & AnotherReallyLongTypeName & YetAnother> = bar;

// Prettier stable
const foo: Promise<SomeReallyLongTypeName &
  AnotherReallyLongTypeName &
  YetAnother> = bar;

// Prettier master
const foo: Promise<
  SomeReallyLongTypeName & AnotherReallyLongTypeName & YetAnother
> = bar;
```
